    "id": "prop:collection-strict-separation",
    "kind": "proposition",
    "statement": "Let \\(0<p_0\\le1\\), \\(0<v_0\\le q_L<q_H\\le1\\), \\(e_0\\le1/2\\), \\(\\rho_0\\le1/4\\), and \\(0<\\mu_n\\le1\\). Then \\(P_n^A,P_n^B\\in\\mathcal M_{4n}\\). They have the same \\(N=4n\\), \\(K=4\\), target composition, aggregate compliance \\(\\mu_D=\\mu_n\\), source sample sizes, source cell masses, propensities, and labeled multiset of per-source prices \\(\\{q_L,q_L,q_H,q_H\\}\\), and neither collection contains a source covering both target cells. Nevertheless, \\[V_{\\mathrm{coll}}(P_n^A,0)=\\frac{q_L+q_H}{8n}>\\frac{q_Lq_H}{2n(q_L+q_H)}=V_{\\mathrm{coll}}(P_n^B,0),\\qquad V_{\\mathrm{coll}}(P_n^A,0)-V_{\\mathrm{coll}}(P_n^B,0)=\\frac{(q_H-q_L)^2}{8n(q_L+q_H)}.\\] Therefore their exact pooled observed-data information bounds \\(1/V_{\\mathrm{coll}}\\), their collection strengths, and their strong-branch honest-length benchmarks are strictly different. No scalar single-source or Kish-style reduction based on pooled size, aggregate compliance, target composition, and marginal per-source precision summaries can represent both collections while retaining observed support; the joint incidence of precision and cell support is necessary.",
